Collection data Type : Python has four built-in collection data types used to store multiple items in a single variable.
Each has a specific personality — ordered vs unordered, mutable vs immutable, unique vs duplicate-allowing — and User can
Choose That  Based On his Requirement.

Type Of Collection Data Type :
1.List
2.Tuple
3.Set
4.Dictionary



1.List : Lists are ordered mutable sequences that can be changed after they have been created by adding, removing, or changing objects.
Lists can be declared by using square brackets “[]” following the variable name.

- Indexing Allow
- Ordaring Allow
- Allow Duplicate Value
- Mutable (We can Perform Insert,Update,Delete)

Real Life Example :
- Grocessary Manage 
- Todo list
- Cricket Match Scoard board

Syntax : S = [1,1,'a',10.8,'abc']
Can Perform Slicing : S[0]
                      S[2:4]
                      S[3: ]
                      S[4]=99

Why : Use a list when you need a collection that:

-Maintains insertion order
-Might grow/shrink over time
-Can contain duplicate values
-Needs to be accessed/modified by index

Pros :

Ordered — index-based access 
Mutable — easy to add/remove/update
Allows duplicates — good for logs, sequences
built-in methods (sort, append, extend, pop)

Cons:

Slower lookups — O(n) check (in operator)
Uses more memory than tuple for same data (mutability overhead)
Not hashable — can't be used as a dictionary key



2.Tuple : Tuples are ordered immutable sequence that stores multiple items in a single variable, meaning it cannot be changed after 
it has been created. A tuple can be created by a pair of parenthesis and comma-separated objects, following the variable name.

Syntax : S = (1,1,'a',10.8,'abc')

- Single object tuples are referred to as a singleton. It can be created by using a trailing comma after the object, or else python 
identifies it as a string.

