Why :

- eliminate duplicates automatically
- super-fast membership testing (in)
- You need mathematical set operations (union, intersection, difference)
- Order doesn't matte

Pros:

Automatically removes duplicates
Extremely fast membership testing — O(1) average
Supports powerful set math (union, intersection, difference, symmetric difference)
Mutable — can add/remove items

Cons:

Unordered — no indexing, no guaranteed order (can't do set[0])
Elements must be hashable (no lists/dicts inside a set)
Not ideal when you need to preserve insertion order or duplicates



4.Dictionary : A dictionary is an unordered set of key/value pairs. Each unique key has a value
- Values in dictionary items can be of any data type.
  Ditionaries are mutable, this allows items to be added or removed even after the dictionary has been created.
  duplicate Keys are Not Allowed in Dictinory , because the values will overwrite and the most recent value will 
  be returned And Value Can be same.

Employ = {"Ravi": "1001", "Het": "1002"}

- X = { "  " : "  "}

- Base on key Index
- Key-Value pair 
- Key must be unique
- Value Duplicate Allow
- Support Json Formate
- Mutable
- Ex TravelGo
     Myntra
     Employ Data
     Student Result

Why :

- to map/associate data (like a real-world lookup table)
- When need fast retrieval by a unique key instead of numeric index
- structured data (like a JSON object

Pros :

- Flexible values (can store lists, dicts, objects, etc.
- maps real-world key→value relationships ( easy to Understand )
- can Access Using Key

Cons :

- More Memory Overhade Than List,Tuple
- No Indexed Based Access
